@*
    One group of the settings menu: a small uppercase label and the pages under
    it, each a link to the page's own address.

    Links rather than buttons, on purpose. A settings page has an address, so
    choosing one is navigation: Back returns to the page before, a page can be
    linked to from anywhere in the app, and the browser's own affordances - open
    in a new window, copy the address - work. The current page is marked with
    aria-current="page", which is what a screen reader announces for it, and
    with the raised surface, which is what everyone else sees - except under a
    frame with no page chosen, where at phone width the menu is the whole screen
    and nothing on it is current (see PageChosen).

    The label is a paragraph the list points at with aria-labelledby, not a
    heading. The menu sits beside the page, and headings there would interleave
    with the page's own outline under its h1.

    A link holds the page's name, its badge value when the host gives it one, and
    a chevron. The chevron is drawn at every width and shown only where the menu
    is the whole screen - the frame's grouped list at phone width, where a row
    reads as "opens a page" - so the markup does not change with the width and
    the frame's stylesheet decides.

    While the settings search has text, a page whose cards it matched lists
    them under its link, each a link of its own to the page with the card named
    in the address's fragment. The host brings that card into view; a link to the
    page already on screen moves only the fragment, so components.js answers that
    one from the link's data-settings-card.
*@

<div class="@ClassValue" data-testid="@TestId" @attributes="AdditionalAttributes">
    <p class="settings-menu-group__label" id="@LabelId">@Label</p>
    <ul class="settings-menu-group__list" aria-labelledby="@LabelId">
        @foreach (var item in Items)
        {
            <li @key="item.Id" class="settings-menu-group__item">
                <a class="@LinkClass(item)"
                   href="@item.Href"
                   data-page="@item.Id"
                   aria-current="@(item.Current && PageChosen ? "page" : null)">
                    <span class="settings-menu-group__name">@item.Label</span>
                    @if (item.Current && !PageChosen)
                    {
                        <span class="sr-only settings-menu-group__current-note"> (current page)</span>
                    }
                    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(item.Badge))
                    {
                        <span class="settings-menu-group__badge" data-testid="settings-menu-badge">@item.Badge</span>
                    }
                    <ChevronUpIcon CssClass="settings-menu-group__chevron" />
                </a>
                @if (item.Cards is { Count: > 0 } cards)
                {
                    <ul class="settings-menu-group__cards" aria-label="@($"Matching cards on {item.Label}")">
                        @foreach (var card in cards)
                        {
                            <li @key="card.Label">
                                <a class="settings-menu-group__card"
                                   href="@card.Href"
                                   data-settings-card="@card.Label"
                                   data-testid="settings-menu-card">@card.Label</a>
                            </li>
                        }
                    </ul>
                }
            </li>
        }
    </ul>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The group's label: Workspace, AI, Product areas.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Label { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The pages in the group, in the order they are listed.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public IReadOnlyList<SettingsMenuItem> Items { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>Whether the frame around the menu has a page chosen - see
    /// <see cref="SettingsFrame.PageChosen"/>. True outside a frame. While it is
    /// false no link carries <c>aria-current</c>: at phone width the menu is the
    /// grouped list with no page on screen. Wider, the default page still sits
    /// beside it, so the current link names itself in a screen-reader note the
    /// phone-width stylesheet takes out of the accessibility tree.</summary>
    [CascadingParameter(Name = SettingsFrame.PageChosenCascade)]
    public bool PageChosen { get; set; } = true;

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)]
    public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object>? AdditionalAttributes { get; set; }

    // Unique per instance, so two groups on one page never label each other's list.
    private readonly string LabelId = "settings-menu-group-" + Guid.NewGuid().ToString("n")[..8];

    private string ClassValue => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(CssClass) ? "settings-menu-group" : $"settings-menu-group {CssClass}";

    private static string LinkClass(SettingsMenuItem item) =>
        item.Current ? "settings-menu-group__link settings-menu-group__link--current" : "settings-menu-group__link";
}
